# Performance Engineering Review

## Findings

### PERF-001: Synchronous Blocking I/O on Every Request in Express Event Loop
**Severity:** Critical
**Location:** Lines 7, 14–16, 21–22

**Evidence:** Every route handler uses synchronous `fs` calls — `fs.readFileSync` (lines 7, 22), `fs.writeFileSync` (line 15), `fs.statSync` (line 16), `fs.readdirSync` (line 21) — inside Express request handlers that run on the Node.js event loop.

**Impact:** Node.js serves all concurrent connections on a single thread. A synchronous file read blocks the entire event loop until the I/O completes. Under any concurrent load, this serializes all requests through disk I/O latency. For example, if `config.json` is on a network filesystem with 5ms latency and 100 requests arrive simultaneously, the last request waits ~500ms just for its turn. The `/logs` endpoint (line 21–22) is especially damaging: it performs `1 + N` synchronous reads (one `readdirSync` + N `readFileSync` calls), blocking the event loop for the entire duration of reading every file in the directory.

**Recommendation:** Replace all synchronous `fs` calls with their async counterparts (or use `fs.promises`):

```typescript
// Before (line 7-9)
app.get("/config/:key", (req, res) => {
  const data = fs.readFileSync("config.json", "utf8");
  const config = JSON.parse(data);
  res.json({ value: config[req.params.key] });
});

// After
import { promises as fsp } from "fs";

app.get("/config/:key", async (req, res) => {
  const data = await fsp.readFile("config.json", "utf8");
  const config = JSON.parse(data);
  res.json({ value: config[req.params.key] });
});
```

Apply the same pattern to `/upload` (use `fsp.writeFile`, `fsp.stat`) and `/logs` (use `fsp.readdir`, `fsp.readFile`). For `/logs`, also consider `Promise.all` for parallel reads once the directory listing is obtained.

