### CYBER-001: LDAP Injection via Unsanitized User Input

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Rule ID** | CYBER-001 |
| **Severity** | **CRITICAL** |
| **CWE** | CWE-90 (LDAP Injection) |
| **OWASP** | A03:2021 – Injection |
| **Location** | Line 6 |

**Description:**

The LDAP search filter on line 6 is constructed via direct string concatenation of user-controlled `username` and `password` parameters:

```java
String filter = "(&(uid=" + username + ")(userPassword=" + password + "))";
```

There is **zero input validation or escaping** applied to either parameter before they are interpolated into the LDAP filter string.

**Exploit Scenario:**

An attacker can bypass authentication entirely by supplying a crafted username:

- **Username:** `*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*`
- **Password:** anything

This produces the filter:
```
(&(uid=*)(uid=*))(|(uid=*)(userPassword=anything))
```

The first filter group `(&(uid=*)(uid=*))` matches **every user** in the directory. Since line 10 checks `results.hasMore()`, authentication succeeds for any attacker without knowing any valid credentials. More targeted attacks can enumerate users, extract attributes, or manipulate query logic arbitrarily.

A simpler bypass:
- **Username:** `*`
- **Password:** `*`

Produces `(&(uid=*)(userPassword=*))` — matches all entries with any uid and any password.

**Remediation:**

Escape all LDAP special characters (`*`, `(`, `)`, `\`, `NUL`, `/`) in user input before filter construction, per RFC 4515. Use a dedicated escaping utility:

```java
import javax.naming.ldap.Rdn;

public boolean authenticate(String username, String password) {
    try {
        // Escape LDAP special characters in user input
        String safeUsername = Rdn.escapeValue(username);
        String safePassword = Rdn.escapeValue(password);
        String filter = "(&(uid=" + safeUsername + ")(userPassword=" + safePassword + "))";
        // ...
    }
}
```

**Even better** — do not include the password in the filter at all. The secure pattern is to search for the user by uid, then **bind** as that user's DN with the supplied password (LDAP bind authentication). Comparing plaintext passwords in a search filter is itself an anti-pattern:

```java
public boolean authenticate(String username, String password) {
    try {
        String safeUsername = Rdn.escapeValue(username);
        String filter = "(uid=" + safeUsername + ")";
        SearchControls sc = new SearchControls();
        sc.setSearchScope(SearchControls.SUBTREE_SCOPE);
        NamingEnumeration<?> results = ctx.search("dc=example,dc=com", filter, sc);
        if (!results.hasMore()) return false;
        String userDn = ((SearchResult) results.next()).getNameInNamespace();

        // Authenticate via LDAP bind
        Hashtable<String, String> env = new Hashtable<>();
        env.put(Context.INITIAL_CONTEXT_FACTORY, "com.sun.jndi.ldap.LdapCtxFactory");
        env.put(Context.PROVIDER_URL, "ldaps://ldap.example.com:636");
        env.put(Context.SECURITY_AUTHENTICATION, "simple");
        env.put(Context.SECURITY_PRINCIPAL, userDn);
        env.put(Context.SECURITY_CREDENTIALS, password);
        new InitialDirContext(env).close();
        return true;
    } catch (AuthenticationException e) {
        return false;
    } catch (NamingException e) {
        return false;
    }
}
```

